    "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-projective-structure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-complex-projective-space-and-holomorphic-charts",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $n\\ge1$, **complex projective space** is the set of complex lines in $\\mathbb C^{n+1}$ with the quotient topology\n$$\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C):=(\\mathbb C^{n+1}\\setminus\\{0\\})/\\mathbb C^\\times,\\qquad [z_0:\\cdots:z_n]:=\\mathbb C^\\times(z_0,\\ldots,z_n).$$\nThe quotient projection is continuous and surjective. Its restriction to the unit sphere $S^{2n+1}\\subset\\mathbb C^{n+1}$ is still surjective, so $\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C)$ is compact. It is Hausdorff: the map sending a nonzero vector $z$ to the orthogonal projection $zz^*/\\lVert z\\rVert^2$ is continuous and constant on each complex line, hence descends by the quotient topology to a continuous injection from $\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C)$ into the Hausdorff space of complex matrices. A continuous injection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism onto its image.\n\nFor open $V\\subseteq\\mathbb C^r$, a map $H:V\\to\\mathbb C^s$ is **holomorphic** if at every $a\\in V$ there is a complex-linear map $A_a:\\mathbb C^r\\to\\mathbb C^s$ such that $H(a+h)=H(a)+A_a h+R_a(h)$ with $\\lVert R_a(h)\\rVert/\\lVert h\\rVert\\to0$ as $h\\to0$, $h\\ne0$. When $r=1$, this is equivalent to each component being holomorphic in the sense of [[def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire]], since a finite vector of scalar difference quotients converges exactly when each component does. A complex atlas consists of charts into $\\mathbb C^n$ with holomorphic transitions in both directions.\n\nFor $j\\in\\{0,\\ldots,n\\}$ let $U_j:=\\{[z_0:\\cdots:z_n]:z_j\\ne0\\}$. These open sets cover projective space: their inverse images under the quotient projection are the open saturated sets where $z_j\\ne0$. The **standard chart** is\n$$\\varphi_j:U_j\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C^n,\\qquad [z_0:\\cdots:z_n]\\longmapsto (z_0/z_j,\\ldots,\\widehat{z_j/z_j},\\ldots,z_n/z_j),$$\nwhere the $j$th coordinate is omitted. It is well defined under rescaling. The coordinate-ratio map on the inverse image of $U_j$ is continuous and constant on each quotient fibre, so the quotient topology makes $\\varphi_j$ continuous; its inverse inserts $1$ in the $j$th position and is continuous by composition with the quotient projection. Hence it is a homeomorphism. On $U_j\\cap U_k$ the transition from the $j$th chart to the $k$th chart sends each coordinate $w_\\ell=z_\\ell/z_j$ ($\\ell\\ne j$) to $w_\\ell/w_k$ for $\\ell\\ne k$, with the omitted $j$th coordinate equal to $1/w_k$. To check holomorphy at $a$ with $a_k\\ne0$, set $a_j=1$ and $h_j=0$ for the omitted source coordinate. Each target coordinate has the expansion $(a_\\ell+h_\\ell)/(a_k+h_k)=a_\\ell/a_k+h_\\ell/a_k-a_\\ell h_k/a_k^2+O(\\lVert h\\rVert^2)$ for $\\ell\\ne k$. The linear term is complex-linear, and the remainder estimate follows by expanding the reciprocal at the nonzero $a_k$. The inverse transition has the same form, so both are holomorphic. Thus these charts make $\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C)$ a complex manifold of complex dimension $n$ and a topological manifold of real dimension $2n$ in the sense of [[def-topological-manifold-without-boundary]]. The finite chart cover by second-countable copies of $\\mathbb C^n$ gives a countable base. Its rational transition functions have smooth real coordinate expressions on their domains, so the atlas also defines the smooth structure in the sense of [[def-smooth-manifold]]. The space is path-connected: distinct lines represented by $u,v$ give the path $t\\mapsto[(1-t)u+tv]$, whose vector never vanishes because $u,v$ are linearly independent; equal lines give a constant path. In particular, $\\mathbb P^1(\\mathbb C)$ is a Riemann surface in the sense of [[def-riemann-surface-and-holomorphic-atlas]].\n\nA map $F:X\\to\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C)$ from a Riemann surface is **holomorphic** when it is continuous and every component of each chart expression $\\varphi_j\\circ F$, written in a local coordinate of $X$, is holomorphic on the open set $F^{-1}(U_j)$. On an overlap, the new components are $f_\\ell/f_k$ and $1/f_k$, where the $f_\\ell$ are the old components and $f_k\\ne0$. The scalar quotient rule gives their holomorphy ([[thm-algebra-of-complex-derivatives]]), so it suffices to check one target chart locally. Changing the source coordinate composes each scalar component with a one-variable holomorphic chart transition, where [[thm-chain-rule-for-complex-derivatives]] applies ([[def-holomorphic-and-meromorphic-map-of-riemann-surfaces]]). A **projective line** is the image in $\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C)$ of a two-dimensional complex subspace of $\\mathbb C^{n+1}$. Every invertible linear map of $\\mathbb C^{n+1}$ induces a holomorphic projective linear transformation: in source and target standard charts its coordinates are ratios of affine-linear functions with nonzero denominator, and the same reciprocal expansion gives a complex-linear derivative; these transformations act transitively on $\\mathbb P^n(\\mathbb C)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qftx-gaga-projective-twisting-line-bundles",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $r\\ge1$, let $\\mathbb P^r(\\mathbb C)$ be the set of complex lines in\n$\\mathbb C^{r+1}$, with its quotient complex-manifold structure, and write\n$[Z_0:\\cdots:Z_r]$ for the line through a nonzero vector\n$(Z_0,\\ldots,Z_r)$. For $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,r\\}$ set\n$U_i=\\{[Z_0:\\cdots:Z_r]:Z_i\\ne0\\}$; its standard coordinates are the ratios\n$Z_j/Z_i$ for $j\\ne i$. For each integer $k$, the **projective twisting line\nbundle** $\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^r}(k)$ is obtained by gluing the trivial\nholomorphic line bundles $U_i\\times\\mathbb C$ with local frames $e_i^{(k)}$\nand transition rule\n$$e_j^{(k)}=\\left(\\frac{Z_j}{Z_i}\\right)^k e_i^{(k)}\\quad\\text{on }U_i\\cap U_j.$$\nThe transition factors are holomorphic and nowhere zero on the overlaps, with\n$g_{ii}=1$, $g_{ij}=g_{ji}^{-1}$, and $g_{ij}g_{j\\ell}=g_{i\\ell}$ on every\ntriple overlap; hence they define a holomorphic line bundle. The sheaf\n$\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^r}(k)$ assigns to an open set the holomorphic sections of\nthis bundle, and $\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^r}:=\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P^r}(0)$ is the\nstructure sheaf.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qftx-gaga-projective-cover-laurent-cohomology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. For $0\\le r\\le3$, let $P^r$ be the complex\nprojective space $(\\mathbb C^{r+1}\\setminus\\{0\\})/\\mathbb C^\\times$ with its\nquotient complex-manifold structure; for $r=0$, $P^0$ is a point. For $r\\ge1$ use the\nstandard homogeneous-coordinate cover $U_i=\\{[Z_0:\\cdots:Z_r]:Z_i\\ne0\\}$,\n$0\\le i\\le r$. For nonempty $I\\subseteq\\{0,\\ldots,r\\}$ write\n$U_I=\\bigcap_{i\\in I}U_i$. For $r\\ge1$ and $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, let\n$\\mathcal O(k)$ be the line bundle with local frames satisfying\n$e_j^{(k)}=(Z_j/Z_i)^k e_i^{(k)}$ on $U_i\\cap U_j$; write\n$\\mathcal O=\\mathcal O(0)$ and $\\mathbb C^*=\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Analytic\nsheaf cohomology $H^q(X,\\mathcal F)$ is the $q$th right-derived\nglobal-sections group of the abelian sheaf $\\mathcal F$. For this ordered cover\nand a sheaf $\\mathcal G$, define\n$$C^p(\\mathcal U,\\mathcal G)=\\prod_{|I|=p+1}\\mathcal G(U_I),\\qquad (\\delta c)_{i_0\\cdots i_{p+1}}=\\sum_{\\nu=0}^{p+1}(-1)^\\nu c_{i_0\\cdots\\widehat{i_\\nu}\\cdots i_{p+1}}|_{U_{i_0\\cdots i_{p+1}}},$$\nfor $0\\le p\\le r$, with $C^p=0$ otherwise and increasing indices;\nfixed-cover Čech cohomology is the cohomology of this complex. A mixed\nLaurent–Taylor series is **normally convergent** when it\nconverges absolutely and uniformly on every compact product subset of its\ndomain. For $r\\ge1$, every\n$U_I$ is biholomorphic to\n$\\mathbb C^{r+1-|I|}\\times(\\mathbb C^*)^{|I|-1}$. For every integer $k$,\neach section on $U_I$, in a standard local frame, has a unique normally\nconvergent mixed Laurent–Taylor expansion indexed by exponent tuples\n$m=(m_0,\\ldots,m_r)\\in\\mathbb Z^{r+1}$ with $\\sum_i m_i=k$; its coefficient\noccurs on $U_I$ exactly when $N(m):=\\{i:m_i<0\\}\\subseteq I$. Restrictions\npreserve each coefficient. In the ordered-cover Čech complex, every mode with\nnonempty proper $N(m)$ has the cochain contraction, for $c\\in C^p$ and an\noutput index $I$ of size $p$, given by $(h_mc)_I=0$ if $j\\in I$ and\n$(h_mc)_I=(-1)^{\\#\\{i\\in I:i<j\\}}c_{I\\cup\\{j\\}}$ if $j\\notin I$, where\n$j$ is the least index outside $N(m)$; the resulting subseries converge\nlocally uniformly on the output intersections. The fixed-cover Čech cohomology\nis canonically the analytic sheaf cohomology whenever that cover is acyclic for\nthe coefficient sheaf. For $k\\in\\{0,-1,-r-1\\}$, the restricted sheaf\n$\\mathcal O(k)|_{U_I}$ has $H^q(U_I,\\mathcal O(k)|_{U_I})=0$ for every integer\n$q>0$, so the cover is acyclic for these three sheaves and the comparison\napplies. Consequently, for $0\\le r\\le3$,\n$$H^0(P^r,\\mathcal O)=\\mathbb C,\\qquad H^q(P^r,\\mathcal O)=0\\quad(q>0),$$\nand for $1\\le r\\le3$,\n$$H^0(P^r,\\mathcal O(-1))=0,\\qquad H^r(P^r,\\mathcal O(-r-1))\\cong\\mathbb C.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-projective-dolbeault-scalar-cohomology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $r\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, let\n$X=\\mathbb P^r(\\mathbb C)=(\\mathbb C^{r+1}\\setminus\\{0\\})/\\mathbb C^\\times$\nwith its standard quotient complex-manifold structure, and let $E\\to X$ be a\nholomorphic vector bundle of finite rank.\nWrite $\\mathcal O(E)$ for its sheaf of holomorphic sections, and let\n$H^q(X,\\mathcal F)$ denote the $q$th right-derived global-sections group of an\nabelian sheaf $\\mathcal F$ on $X$. For\n$0\\le q\\le r$, let $\\mathcal A^{0,q}(E)$ be the sheaf of smooth sections of\n$\\Lambda^{0,q}T^*X\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}E$, where $\\Lambda^{0,q}T^*X$ is the\n$q$th exterior power of the antiholomorphic cotangent subbundle. In a\nholomorphic local frame of $E$,\ndefine $\\bar\\partial_E$ by applying the scalar $\\bar\\partial$ to each\ncoefficient form; holomorphic transition matrices make this definition\nindependent of frame, and $\\bar\\partial_E^2=0$. Set\n$\\mathcal A^{0,q}(E)=0$ for $q>r$ and define\n$$H^q_{\\bar\\partial}(X,E):=\\frac{\\ker\\bigl(\\bar\\partial_E:\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal A^{0,q}(E))\\to\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal A^{0,q+1}(E))\\bigr)}{\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(\\bar\\partial_E:\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal A^{0,q-1}(E))\\to\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal A^{0,q}(E))\\bigr)},$$\nwhere $\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal F)$ denotes the group of sections of $\\mathcal F$ over\n$X$ and the denominator is zero for $q=0$. For every $q\\ge0$, the canonical\ncomparison induced by the smooth Dolbeault resolution is an isomorphism\n$$H^q(X,\\mathcal O(E))\\cong H^q_{\\bar\\partial}(X,E).$$\nFor the trivial line bundle $E=\\mathcal O_X$ and $0\\le r\\le3$, this gives\n$$H^0(\\mathbb P^r,\\mathcal O)=\\mathbb C,\\qquad H^q(\\mathbb P^r,\\mathcal O)=0\\quad(q>0).$$\nThe scalar cohomology conclusion is obtained by the direct Laurent–Čech\ncalculation on the standard projective cover; no GAGA theorem, Kähler Hodge\ntheorem in dimensions above one, or arbitrary-coherent projective vanishing is\nassumed.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The degree-zero exponents satisfy $m_0+m_1+m_2=0$. If $N(m)=\\varnothing$, all three exponents are nonnegative, so their sum is zero only for $m=(0,0,0)$. If $N(m)=\\{0,1,2\\}$, all three are negative and their sum is negative, impossible. Thus every other mode has nonempty proper negative set, one of the six sets $\\{0\\},\\{1\\},\\{2\\},\\{0,1\\},\\{0,2\\},\\{1,2\\}$. The least vertex outside these sets is respectively $1,0,0,2,1,0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each nonzero mode choose its least $j\\notin N(m)$. On an increasing output index $I$ of size $p$, put $(h_mc)_I=0$ if $j\\in I$ and otherwise put $(h_mc)_I=(-1)^{\\#\\{i\\in I:i<j\\}}c_{I\\cup\\{j\\}}$. For an input index $I$ of size $p+1$ with $j\\notin I$, the term of $h_m\\delta c$ deleting $j$ has the same sign as the insertion sign, hence contributes $+c_I$; for every $i\\in I$, the term deleting $i$ in $h_m\\delta c$ and the term deleting $i$ in $\\delta h_mc$ have opposite signs because the order of inserting $j$ and deleting $i$ is reversed. If $j\\in I$, then $h_m\\delta c$ vanishes and the only nonzero term in $\\delta h_mc$ deletes $j$; its two signs agree and give $+c_I$. Terms deleting a vertex of $N(m)$ vanish because the resulting index does not contain $N(m)$. Therefore $\\delta h_m+h_m\\delta=\\mathrm{id}$ in every degree for all six possible $N(m)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "By [F2], the zero mode of the structure sheaf is a scalar constant on each nonempty intersection. Its Čech complex is $C^0=\\mathbb C^3$, $C^1=\\mathbb C^3$, $C^2=\\mathbb C$, with maps $\\delta^0(c_0,c_1,c_2)=(c_1-c_0,c_2-c_0,c_2-c_1)$ and $\\delta^1(a_{01},a_{02},a_{12})=a_{12}-a_{02}+a_{01}$. The kernel of $\\delta^0$ consists exactly of $(c,c,c)$, hence is $\\mathbb C$. The image of $\\delta^0$ is the plane $a_{02}=a_{01}+a_{12}$, which is exactly the kernel of $\\delta^1$; $\\delta^1$ is onto because $(0,0,1)$ maps to $1$. Thus this mode has $H^0=\\mathbb C$ and $H^1=H^2=0$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The modewise formula defines a homotopy on actual holomorphic cochains: by [F3], each output component is a sum of subseries grouped by at most the three choices of $j\\notin I$, each retained mode has no negative exponent at the added coordinate, and the subseries converge locally uniformly on $U_I$. Consequently their sums are holomorphic on $U_I$, including across the added coordinate hyperplane, and the coefficient identity in step 2.1 is an identity of analytic cochains.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Every nonzero mode is contractible by steps 1.1, 2.1 and 3.1, while the zero mode has the cohomology computed in step 2.2. The unique normally convergent coefficient expansions and their cochain homotopies make this coefficientwise computation valid for holomorphic sections, and [F3] identifies the resulting fixed-cover Čech cohomology with analytic sheaf cohomology. Hence the displayed sheaf-cohomology values follow.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F4] to the trivial line bundle $E=\\mathcal O_X$. Its global smooth Dolbeault complex has the same cohomology as the sheaf groups computed in step 4.1, giving the asserted Dolbeault-cohomology values.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The standard Čech complex is indexed only by nonempty chart subsets; modes with empty negative set are isolated in step 1.1."
