      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-untwisted-obstruction-1-1",
          "claim": "A global section restricts to every singleton chart $U_i$. Its coefficient mode $m$ must therefore occur on every $U_i$, so [F4] requires $N(m)\\subseteq\\{i\\}$ for all $i$. Since $r\\ge1$, there are at least two distinct singleton charts, whose intersection as index sets is empty; hence $N(m)=\\varnothing$ and every $m_i\\ge0$. For total degree $-1$, the equation $\\sum_i m_i=-1$ is then impossible. Thus the degree-zero Čech cocycles for $\\mathcal O(-1)$ are zero, and the fixed-cover comparison in [F4] gives $H^0(X,\\mathcal O(-1))=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-untwisted-obstruction-2-1",
          "claim": "The top Čech cochains have the single index set $J=\\{0,\\ldots,r\\}$. A degree-$k$ mode appears there with no restriction beyond $N(m)\\subseteq J$. It is a top coboundary exactly when its negative set is contained in at least one codimension-one face $J\\setminus\\{j\\}$, equivalently when $N(m)\\ne J$: one implication follows because the input cochain on that face has no coefficient unless $N(m)\\subseteq J\\setminus\\{j\\}$; conversely, if $N(m)\\ne J$, choose $j\\in J\\setminus N(m)$ and the contraction of [F4] supplies a preimage in that face. Therefore top cohomology has exactly the modes with $N(m)=J$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-untwisted-obstruction-3-1",
          "claim": "For $k=-r-1$, if every $m_i<0$, write $m_i=-1-n_i$ with integers $n_i\\ge0$. The total-degree equation becomes $-(r+1)-\\sum_i n_i=-(r+1)$, so $\\sum_i n_i=0$ and every $n_i=0$. Thus the only surviving mode is $m=(-1,\\ldots,-1)$, and its coefficient space is $\\mathbb C$. The normal convergence and analytic cochain contraction in [F4] show that this coefficient quotient is the actual analytic Čech quotient. The comparison in [F4] now gives $H^r(X,\\mathcal O(-r-1))\\cong\\mathbb C$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-untwisted-obstruction-4-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F5] to both holomorphic line bundles $\\mathcal O(-1)$ and $\\mathcal O(-r-1)$. The canonical isomorphisms identify their global Dolbeault cohomology with the sheaf groups computed in steps 1.1 and 3.1, so the Dolbeault values are zero in degree zero for $\\mathcal O(-1)$ and $\\mathbb C$ in degree $r$ for $\\mathcal O(-r-1)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
